{"number": 39, "text": "                   ‘hair-raising             and     hard-'working\n                  Stress       in  compound             adjectives          and     in   abbreviations\n\n                  A compound    adjective is a fixed expression which is made up of more than one word and which\n                  has the function of an adjective. Most compound  adjectives are written with a hyphen, but a few\n                  are written as one word:\n                         skin-deep           long-term          threadbare\n\n              4°\" The  following types of compound  adjective usually have main stress on the first part:\n                  e compound   adjectives usually written as one word\n                         ‘airtight           ‘carefree          ‘praise, worthy\n                         Exceptions: ,nation'wide, ,hand'made\n                  e noun  + -ing form\n                                                  paying        ‘time-con,suming\n                  e noun  + past participle\n\n                         Exceptions: ,eagle-'eyed,\n              A82\n                  The  following types of compound  adjective usually have main stress on the second part:\n                  e noun  + adjective\n                         fat-'free           isky-‘high                        (and other colour compounds)\n                         Exception: 'camera-shy\n                  e adjective + noun\n                                             full-'length\n                  e adverb or adjective + past participle\n                         fully-'grown        long-'sighted\n                  e adverb or adjective + -ing form\n\n                         Exceptions: 'backward-,looking, 'forward-,looking\n                  e self- as the first part\n                         self-'confident     self-in'flicted    self-'governing\n\n                  Most  compound   adjectives with main stress on the second part (including the exceptions in 17B)\n                  can have stress shift (see Unit 10B). Compare:\n                         The tiger was fully-GROWN.     but: It was a FULly-grown  Tlger.\n                         The prices were sky-HIGH.    but: They were  SKY-high  PRIces.\n\n\n                  Two-,  three- and four-letter abbreviations said as individual letters often have main stress on the\n                  last letter and secondary stress on the first:\n                         the             the ,U'K        the ,BB'C        DN'A         the ,YMC'A\n\n                  Abbreviations like this usually have stress shift. Compare:\n                         He works  for the BBC.  but: He works  for BBc RAdio.\n                         She’s from the UK.  but: She’s a UK\n\n\n                     English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 2759, "mean_conf": 87.09, "word_count": 280, "words": [{"text": "‘A81:", "x0": 262, "y0": 605, "x1": 331, "y1": 643, "conf": 0, "bold": false}, {"text": "‘hair-raising", "x0": 363, "y0": 113, "x1": 835, "y1": 196, "conf": 59, "bold": false}, {"text": "and", "x0": 874, "y0": 112, "x1": 1003, "y1": 176, "conf": 76, "bold": false}, {"text": "hard-'working", "x0": 1047, "y0": 112, "x1": 1592, "y1": 196, "conf": 76, "bold": false}, {"text": "Stress", "x0": 357, "y0": 205, "x1": 565, "y1": 269, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "in", "x0": 601, "y0": 206, "x1": 659, "y1": 269, "conf": 94, "bold": false}, {"text": "compound", "x0": 694, "y0": 206, "x1": 1066, "y1": 288, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "adjectives", "x0": 1101, "y0": 205, "x1": 1450, "y1": 289, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "and", "x0": 1483, "y0": 206, "x1": 1610, "y1": 269, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "in", "x0": 1647, "y0": 206, "x1": 1705, "y1": 269, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "abbreviations", "x0": 1740, "y0": 206, "x1": 2220, "y1": 269, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "A", "x0": 354, "y0": 351, "x1": 384, "y1": 384, "conf": 94, "bo